      "courseId": "urn:uuid:d245f1fe-e98e-4760-a18d-48d8f30182e5",
      "category": "definition",
      "question": "What is the Malthusian trap?",
      "expected": "answered",
      "note": "Activity 2 names the Malthusian trap. It does not define it.",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "latencyMs": 1102,
      "answer": "The Malthusian trap is a concept from classical growth theory associated with Malthus. It describes how population growth tends to outpace food production because of diminishing returns, especially from land. As population increases, the limited supply of land leads to lower output per worker, so incomes are pushed back down to subsistence levels. This traps the economy in a cycle where growth in population offsets any gains in output, preventing sustained improvements in living standards.",
      "basis": null,
      "refusal": null,
      "hostSentRefusal": false,
      "error": null,
      "grounded": true,
      "refused": false,
